Sheng's recovery of a core whose start-up self-IPI was lost (sheng_sigp_recover: SIGPdisabled cleared by the signalling core or the
idle loop) is off unless sheng-sigp-recover=1. A DroidVM guest loses no IPI (ArmCpu delivers them as SPIs), and the recovery cleared
SIGPdisabled of the first secondary between its start and processor_cpu_reinit(), which asserts it is still set.

diff --git a/osfmk/arm/cpu_common.c b/osfmk/arm/cpu_common.c
index 8776f16..fbcaf99 100644
--- a/osfmk/arm/cpu_common.c
+++ b/osfmk/arm/cpu_common.c
@@ -32,6 +32,7 @@
  */
 
 #include <kern/machine.h>
+#include <kern/startup.h>
 #include <kern/cpu_number.h>
 #include <kern/thread.h>
 #include <kern/percpu.h>
@@ -413,9 +414,19 @@ cpu_immediate_xcall(int cpu_number, broadcastFunc func, void *param)
  * and UFS root failures under SMP: the I/O thread's wakeup IPI to that core was silently dropped). A core in InitState|StartedState runs and
  * takes interrupts, so the flag is cleared here and the signal sent. Counted in sheng_sigp_recovered. */
 volatile uint32_t sheng_sigp_recovered;
+/*
+ * DroidVM: off unless sheng-sigp-recover=1. A guest's IPIs, its start-up self-IPI included, reach every vCPU (ArmCpu's SPI path), so
+ * nothing is lost to recover; and the recovery clears SIGPdisabled of a core that machine_cpu_reinit() has started but whose
+ * processor_cpu_reinit() has not yet enabled interrupts, when another core signals it in that window (an AST at the first secondary's
+ * start: "Assertion failed: getCpuDatap()->cpu_signal == SIGPdisabled (0x8 == 0x80000000)").
+ */
+static TUNABLE(bool, sheng_sigp_recovery, "sheng-sigp-recover", false);
 bool
 sheng_sigp_recover(cpu_data_t *target_proc)
 {
+	if (!sheng_sigp_recovery) {
+		return false;
+	}
 	if ((os_atomic_load(&target_proc->cpu_flags, relaxed) & (InitState | StartedState)) != (InitState | StartedState)) {
 		return false;
 	}
